When does orthogonal-by-construction recover the TRUE parameters? A sympy derivation.

Maarten Schoukens, 2026-09-18: orthogonal projection makes the split between baseline and
augmentation UNIQUE, but it recovers the TRUE physical parameters only if the true missing
dynamics are themselves orthogonal to the baseline's parameter sensitivities. This script
establishes that claim symbolically, in the order the numerical companion then follows.

Notation, fixed here and used by `measure_delta_orthogonality.py` and by
`baseline-and-added-dynamics.md`:

    f_base(theta)   the discrete one-step baseline map, stacked over the reference set,
                    as a vector in R^n  (n = N_samples * n_protected_rows)
    theta*          the true physical parameter vector, R^p
    Delta*          the TRUE missing dynamics on the reference set, R^n,
                    defined by  f_truth = f_base(theta*) + Delta*
    J               d f_base / d theta evaluated at the expansion point, R^{n x p}
    P = J J^+       the orthogonal projector onto range(J)
    f_ann           the learning component evaluated on the same reference set, R^n
    dtheta          theta_hat - theta*, the parameter bias

The OBC constraint is  J^T f_ann = 0,  i.e. f_ann in range(J)^perp.

Five results are produced:

  R1  affine baseline, exact interpolation: dtheta = J^+ Delta*, and dtheta = 0 iff J^T Delta* = 0.
  R2  affine baseline, LEAST SQUARES with a CAPACITY-LIMITED f_ann: the same dtheta = J^+ Delta*,
      exactly, independent of what the network can represent. This is stronger than R1 and it is
      what makes the predicted bias a prediction rather than a bound.
  R3  baseline nonlinear in theta: the identity holds to first order, with an explicit
      second-order remainder -1/2 J^+ H[dtheta, dtheta].
  R4  basis built at a WRONG expansion point theta_0: the projection becomes oblique and
      dtheta = (J_0^T J)^{-1} J_0^T Delta*. This is why the numerical companion reports the
      answer at both the true and the detuned parameter point.
  R5  rank deficiency: with rank(J) = r < p the bias is only determined modulo ker(J), and the
      minimum-norm representative is J^+ Delta*.

==============================================================================
R1  AFFINE BASELINE, EXACT INTERPOLATION
==============================================================================
Truth       : f_truth = f_base(theta*) + Delta*
Model       : f_base(theta*) + J dtheta + f_ann          (f_base affine in theta)
Interpolate : J dtheta + f_ann = Delta*                  ... (1)
OBC         : J^T f_ann = 0                              ... (2)

Left-multiply (1) by J^T and use (2):  (J^T J) dtheta = J^T Delta*.
With J of full column rank, J^T J is invertible, hence

    dtheta = (J^T J)^-1 J^T Delta* = J^+ Delta*.         ... (3)

CHECK (existence)  residual of (1) at dtheta = J^+ Delta*, f_ann = (I - P) Delta*:
       Matrix([[0, 0, 0, 0]])
CHECK (existence)  residual of (2), i.e. J^T (I - P) Delta*:
       Matrix([[0, 0]])
       -> both zero.

UNIQUENESS.  The map (dtheta, f_ann) -> (J dtheta + f_ann, J^T f_ann) is injective when
J has full column rank: if J dtheta + f_ann = 0 and J^T f_ann = 0, then multiplying the
first by J^T gives (J^T J) dtheta = 0, hence dtheta = 0, hence f_ann = 0. So the pair
above is THE solution, and the split is UNIQUE. That uniqueness is the property
projection was introduced for. Uniqueness is not correctness.

CHECK  P^T = P, P^2 = P, P J = J, J^T (I - P) = 0: all verified symbolically.

THE CONDITION.  J has full column rank, so J^+ Delta* = 0 iff J^T Delta* = 0:
  (=>) J^+ Delta* = 0  =>  J J^+ Delta* = P Delta* = 0  =>  J^T Delta* = J^T P Delta* = 0
  (<=) J^T Delta* = 0  =>  (J^T J)^-1 J^T Delta* = 0.

    theta_hat = theta*   <==>   J^T Delta* = 0   <==>   Delta* PERPENDICULAR range(J).

Equivalently, with rho* = ||P Delta*|| / ||Delta*|| in [0, 1]:
    rho* = 0  <==>  no parameter bias.   rho* > 0  ==>  a bias of size
    ||dtheta|| = ||J^+ Delta*|| >= rho* ||Delta*|| / sigma_max(J).

    rho* ||Delta*|| / sigma_max(J)  <=  ||dtheta||  <=  rho* ||Delta*|| / sigma_min(J)
    ... so rho* alone does NOT fix the bias magnitude; the conditioning of J does too.
    This is why the numerical companion reports J^+ Delta* itself, not only rho*.

==============================================================================
R2  LEAST SQUARES WITH A CAPACITY-LIMITED AUGMENTATION
==============================================================================
R1 assumed the model can interpolate Delta* exactly. A three-layer, 24-node network
cannot. Let A be ANY subspace of reachable augmentation fields, and let OBC enforce
A subset of range(J)^perp. The training problem is

    min over dtheta in R^p, f_ann in A   || J dtheta + f_ann - Delta* ||^2.

Split Delta* = P Delta* + (I - P) Delta*. Then J dtheta lies in range(J) and both
f_ann and (I - P) Delta* lie in range(J)^perp, so the two parts are orthogonal and
Pythagoras applies term by term:

    || J dtheta - P Delta* ||^2  +  || f_ann - (I - P) Delta* ||^2.     ... (4)

  (J dtheta)^T f_ann = dtheta^T (J^T f_ann) = 0
      by the OBC constraint itself  [algebraic]
  (J dtheta)^T (I - P) D = dtheta^T J^T (I - P) D = 0
      because J^T (I - P) = 0  [symbolically verified]
  (P D)^T f_ann = D^T P f_ann = D^T J (J^T J)^-1 (J^T f_ann) = 0
      again by the constraint, since P factors through J^T  [algebraic]
  (P D)^T (I - P) D = D^T P (I - P) D = 0
      because P (I - P) = 0  [symbolically verified]

       -> the cross term is identically zero, so (4) holds.

CONSEQUENCE.  The first term of (4) does not contain f_ann and the second does not
contain dtheta, so the minimisation SEPARATES. The parameter minimiser is

    dtheta = argmin || J dtheta - P Delta* ||^2 = J^+ P Delta* = J^+ Delta*,

which is EXACTLY R1, whatever A is. Limited network capacity degrades the FIT, by
|| f_ann - (I - P) Delta* ||, and leaves the parameter bias untouched.

So J^+ Delta* is a PREDICTION of the parameter error under OBC, not a lower bound.
Caveats that break the equality in the real training problem, in order of size:
  (a) the objective is a multi-step CLOSED-LOOP rollout, not the one-step least
      squares on the reference set that defines J; the two weight samples and rows
      differently, so the effective inner product is not the one OBC orthogonalises in;
  (b) the constraint holds at the CURRENT expansion point, which moves every epoch;
  (c) the baseline is not affine in theta (R3);
  (d) optimisation need not reach the minimiser in the budget.

==============================================================================
R3  BASELINE NONLINEAR IN THETA: THE SECOND-ORDER REMAINDER
==============================================================================
Write the exact Taylor expansion of the baseline about theta*, with H the (n x p x p)
second derivative and H[a, b] the vector with entries sum_jk H_ijk a_j b_k:

    f_base(theta* + dtheta) = f_base(theta*) + J dtheta + 1/2 H[dtheta, dtheta] + O(|dtheta|^3)

Interpolation and the OBC constraint now read
    J dtheta + 1/2 H[dtheta, dtheta] + f_ann = Delta*,     J^T f_ann = 0,
so left-multiplying by J^T,

    (J^T J) dtheta = J^T Delta* - 1/2 J^T H[dtheta, dtheta]
    dtheta = J^+ Delta* - 1/2 J^+ H[dtheta, dtheta].                 ... (5)

Substituting dtheta = J^+ Delta* + O(||Delta*||^2) into the remainder of (5):

    dtheta = J^+ Delta*  -  1/2 J^+ H[J^+ Delta*, J^+ Delta*]  +  O(||Delta*||^3).  ... (6)

CHECK  order eps  (first-order term) against J^+ Delta*:
       0
       -> zero.
CHECK  order eps^2 (remainder) against -1/2 J^+ H[J^+ Delta*, J^+ Delta*]:
       0
       -> zero. Equation (6) is confirmed.

SIZE OF THE REMAINDER, relative to the leading term:
    || 1/2 J^+ H[dtheta, dtheta] || / || J^+ Delta* ||  ~  1/2 kappa ||dtheta||,
with kappa = ||J^+|| ||H|| a curvature-to-sensitivity ratio with units of 1/theta.
For the gantry the nine log coordinates give H = J elementwise in the scalar
directions (d^2/dv^2 exp(v) = d/dv exp(v)), so kappa ~ 1 in the free coordinate and
the relative remainder is about ||dtheta||/2. At the run's 9.5 percent initial
detuning that is roughly 5 percent OF the bias, not 5 percentage points: it cannot
turn a 2 percent prediction into a 7 percent observation.

==============================================================================
R4  A BASIS BUILT AT THE WRONG EXPANSION POINT
==============================================================================
In training the basis is refreshed at the CURRENT parameter point theta_0, not at the
unknown theta*. The constraint is then J_0^T f_ann = 0 while the parameter direction
the model actually moves along is J = d f_base / d theta at theta*. Interpolation:

    J dtheta + f_ann = Delta*,     J_0^T f_ann = 0
    => (J_0^T J) dtheta = J_0^T Delta*
    => dtheta = (J_0^T J)^-1 J_0^T Delta*.                            ... (7)

CHECK (existence)  residual of the interpolation at (7):
       Matrix([[0, 0, 0, 0]])
CHECK (existence)  residual of the constraint J_0^T f_ann = 0 at (7):
       Matrix([[0, 0]])
       -> both zero; uniqueness follows as in R1 with J_0^T J invertible.

CHECK  J (J_0^T J)^-1 J_0^T is idempotent: verified symbolically.
       It is NOT symmetric unless range(J_0) = range(J).

(7) is an OBLIQUE projection: J (J_0^T J)^-1 J_0^T is idempotent but not symmetric.
It reduces to J^+ when J_0 = J. Two consequences:
  (i)  the vanishing condition becomes J_0^T Delta* = 0, a condition on the basis
       actually used, not on the truth basis;
  (ii) the bias can be LARGER than the orthogonal one, because (J_0^T J)^-1 is not
       bounded by 1/sigma_min(J) when the two column spaces are misaligned.
This is why the numerical companion builds J at BOTH theta* and the detuned theta_0
and reports the gap between the two predictions as the expansion-point sensitivity.

==============================================================================
R5  RANK DEFICIENCY
==============================================================================
If rank(J) = r < p, then J^T J is singular and (3) has no unique solution: any
dtheta + v with v in ker(J) interpolates equally well, and the fit cannot distinguish
them. Projection does not help, because ker(J) directions leave f_base unchanged to
first order and therefore leave the orthogonality constraint unchanged too.

    dtheta in J^+ Delta* + ker(J),   minimum-norm representative J^+ Delta*.

The gantry basis is FULL rank 10 of 10 at condition 8.9e+02, so this branch is not
active here. It is stated because it is the second way OBC can fail to recover theta*,
and it is the one the ten-combination reduction (D-190) was introduced to remove: the
fourteen raw scalars give rank 10 and nullity 4, where every statement above is void
on four directions.

==============================================================================
SUMMARY
==============================================================================
R1  dtheta = J^+ Delta*,  and theta_hat = theta* iff J^T Delta* = 0.   [exact, affine]
R2  the same dtheta holds under least squares with ANY capacity-limited f_ann,
    because the objective separates into a range(J) part and a range(J)^perp part.
R3  dtheta = J^+ Delta* - 1/2 J^+ H[J^+ Delta*, J^+ Delta*] + O(||Delta*||^3).
R4  with the basis at theta_0: dtheta = (J_0^T J)^-1 J_0^T Delta*, oblique.
R5  with rank(J) < p the bias is fixed only modulo ker(J).

The two numbers to measure:
    rho*    = ||P Delta*|| / ||Delta*||     how non-orthogonal the truth actually is
    J^+ Delta*                              the predicted parameter bias, per parameter
Both are computed by measure_delta_orthogonality.py in this folder.
